#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417cda45-5deb-5a71-9253-a3a0df6b4f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407366} {:answer/id #uuid "47984d83-a59d-5c9e-b0ed-fecc2f753d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407368} {:answer/id #uuid "852beed3-2c95-5176-a9ae-504c72adf621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407395} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3d09f0-2c82-592d-bfc6-7d6d6263bbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407403} {:answer/id #uuid "ad69e9c1-1ebb-560c-9e77-00f1fd732382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417cda45-5deb-5a71-9253-a3a0df6b4f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407366} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfbeec0-d695-5669-98f1-e0c98afcce16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57bcbc75-d195-518f-bc0e-7dcf10346726" :question/math-academy-id "q-221924" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $2∣(60x-45y)$\n- $9∣(60x-45y)$\n- $5∣(60x-45y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2272ac5e-7d0f-56a3-ac97-994658312363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407354} {:answer/id #uuid "2b69be42-2b62-50f4-b509-868d1c9cc7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407356} {:answer/id #uuid "990a7a87-e36c-55af-8157-7626787e9d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407409} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c277c5-5a7b-5cdc-9f28-011af8d694cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407418} {:answer/id #uuid "d24532ad-ad89-5f9d-ae39-b1ba94e8bf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b69be42-2b62-50f4-b509-868d1c9cc7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407356} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12c464c-0427-5713-9930-41823b0ab244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "677c3e63-2822-579f-a67a-69517e4d5770" :question/math-academy-id "q-221927" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $5∣(8x+6y)$\n- $2∣(8x+6y)$\n- $3∣(8x+6y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n8x+6y=2(4x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(8x+6y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c4c39ff-1830-583c-bfbe-3b1923927d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407389} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4b945a-cfab-5802-9826-b499d69bf07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407405} {:answer/id #uuid "8fef28c7-794b-5733-a1fe-0753d63b034f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407407} {:answer/id #uuid "bf65c1a0-9ca7-5e13-851e-5b8ebe65ec4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407429} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e88934-c419-5f9c-aeef-75af01952dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fef28c7-794b-5733-a1fe-0753d63b034f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407407} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2706c2-73db-5a55-a6d5-9289ff01e1ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87475b8f-4c6f-5041-abc0-cc4d71389ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-221928" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $3$ divides $(8x+6y)$\n- $2$ divides $(8x+6y)$\n- $4$ divides $(8x+6y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $8(1)+6(0)=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n8x+6y=2(4x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(8x+6y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $8(0)+6(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070efb65-293c-5069-a098-e98293361bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407341} {:answer/id #uuid "262239e2-54ef-5ba5-9c45-43c82a528c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407355} {:answer/id #uuid "59b4b9f4-8423-53e2-86a4-af6400356c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407375} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8a7ed1-a714-5a9e-8cca-5f810fde21a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407379} {:answer/id #uuid "c203c295-5f0c-5cb5-a465-33ad0fef7926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59b4b9f4-8423-53e2-86a4-af6400356c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e816fd2-d475-50e3-9740-ad25b3cf43dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7df593d-9767-5ee4-9c1c-cf41d12d4aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-221932" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $6$ divides $(24x+30y)$\n- $3$ divides $(24x+30y)$\n- $5$ divides $(24x+30y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=6(4x+5y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+5y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=3(8x+10y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $8x+10y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have $24(-1)+30(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327bdd4d-a7b7-544a-8fe3-29b96fdae097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407747} {:answer/id #uuid "90adef6d-ebf9-5022-b425-c00ccc41e262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407756} {:answer/id #uuid "b739d2d3-c9a6-54bf-a835-cce7edf2bbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407760} {:answer/id #uuid "e691e6b5-6293-530b-8086-ebf6e664e768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407765} {:answer/id #uuid "f67c8a35-ab46-557b-8394-b8e6ccc7dd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b739d2d3-c9a6-54bf-a835-cce7edf2bbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407760} :answer-field/id #uuid "79ed2cb4-0480-56a4-83ba-daa7fd0af2da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5252a0f-1ef3-54ce-a8b5-332d495a5706" :question/math-academy-id "q-94782" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\text{ }{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent one, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${v}_{2}+{v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{1}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{3}$ can not be a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}.$ As a result ${v}_{2}+{v}_{3}$ can't be written as combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$ too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "320c4694-1904-5fc7-a324-a4c89f0e3257" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58e92df-9945-5743-8088-4250afd4e285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407794} {:answer/id #uuid "c523af15-9d38-59e1-a24b-c6e9af255c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407796} {:answer/id #uuid "c64e638f-c63c-542f-96c1-65992079bca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407797} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ba58d-5e8a-5213-afcf-34f6a5938516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407799} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf6d984-9d9e-5c92-a6cf-995e484dfb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58e92df-9945-5743-8088-4250afd4e285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407794} :answer-field/id #uuid "589eb3e5-2666-5827-8cea-4a656b6bf3b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041632fd-d218-5815-9c69-da107f5da160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407822} {:answer/id #uuid "091e3fce-c81a-50bf-8d66-819204f1ee13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407823} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84526b-47f3-51fb-a168-2cba08c88949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407830} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aeae6b-4c5c-573f-8a2d-c1af8e88ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407840} {:answer/id #uuid "f5036257-dfe1-524a-b3ae-379280f91c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7aeae6b-4c5c-573f-8a2d-c1af8e88ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407840} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b798538-abe6-5e7d-a0f2-143f5a975750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27143f05-01a0-516c-bb47-d27121720b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407859} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4e93b6-1460-51b3-854b-d70d0185543b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407862} {:answer/id #uuid "5f61eda3-2ef3-5e67-a98c-6effd83ff8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407865} {:answer/id #uuid "76f0dd4e-ff74-50db-9f41-52a58331f2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407867} {:answer/id #uuid "9b94f507-0ee4-574c-849e-434a6fd9142d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b94f507-0ee4-574c-849e-434a6fd9142d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407873} :answer-field/id #uuid "6472838c-210f-5ad0-99a2-67a0bbd5ad6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a3dbd42-cd16-5083-8842-33f0028feb3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105207" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-2,4]\\to [-3,9]$ which is given by $f(x)=2x+1?$\n\n- $f(-2)=f(4)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5f/5fd931a87d6a5fb32d10ed931f443a8175f0760eba9fe2fe69336f8f79e9fd68.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $f(2)=2(-2)+1=-3$ and $f(4)=2\\cdot 4+1=9.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[-2,4]$ that map to the same element in $[-3,9].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f82104-dc7d-57c3-98d6-01a9c65196e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408025} {:answer/id #uuid "322e2b30-d710-51d2-8606-8bee18140367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408026} {:answer/id #uuid "59ad5255-5a70-5c33-9335-d0a5e5d59557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408030} {:answer/id #uuid "6249b9e8-0127-5eee-876d-bd9fe3be9480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408031} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d72fdb-d63f-5f10-a2b0-b041b91327b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6249b9e8-0127-5eee-876d-bd9fe3be9480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408031} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77bfd50-03ce-5cb3-a091-5ba8b32f073a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b46ac655-a97e-50cb-a022-2af841ed15e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160451" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152073 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "398716c2-d0e6-5106-a702-2f71d957726f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a23adb-0758-5db2-acbc-ce4a08c7bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409355} {:answer/id #uuid "45739530-0f38-5422-9dc2-6ee62cb4fa98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409364} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbe99c-cc95-5126-be17-7b1302dafd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409370} {:answer/id #uuid "ab117369-f4ee-599c-af58-4abaa57df29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409396} {:answer/id #uuid "e01ed3c3-d611-527c-a4cd-eed95fbee3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a23adb-0758-5db2-acbc-ce4a08c7bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409355} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e7547b-ae8c-5644-a80c-add06b2c90c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a4a4ae5-eba9-597a-b12c-2ce503fbc86a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103385" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $18≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484f4bca-d64b-544b-b87d-1bacf4c35986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409365} {:answer/id #uuid "48f7b955-4755-5e56-9f1c-71e139699c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409366} {:answer/id #uuid "6e577ca3-4903-5b1b-a794-47a45d1f0c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409376} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc3ad2-783a-5d36-ba03-cfd9ee2be154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409407} {:answer/id #uuid "e318448a-9322-5d40-bf1b-76ebf9029ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc3ad2-783a-5d36-ba03-cfd9ee2be154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409407} :answer-field/id #uuid "a610aa87-901f-5a33-b3da-57a6452cd19a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42227076-6750-5c0c-9261-152604f91699" :question/math-academy-id "q-76953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $17≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$\n- $23≡-10\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $33≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $9∤(17-2).$ Notice that $17-2=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $5∤(23-(-10)).$ Notice that $23-(-10)=33,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(33-12).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(33-12)÷7=21÷7=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "621c44d6-93dc-5220-ad30-48313fff1042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409372} {:answer/id #uuid "86dcace0-8e17-596b-b607-813bfd8841f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409382} {:answer/id #uuid "93692f71-aa41-54d8-9db3-7599da38199e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409384} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c696b6-e407-551e-9c85-750416b8ebf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409404} {:answer/id #uuid "e828b90d-adc9-50db-9dff-69261bedddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e828b90d-adc9-50db-9dff-69261bedddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409415} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90945b6-ae5d-503f-bb6d-c321c3cb1593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae89ee49-3f2d-51e4-a2f6-ac3e918a674e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76958" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $12≡36\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$\n- $-9≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $21≡45\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $2∣(12-36).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(12-36)÷2=-24÷2=-12\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $4∤(-9-18).$ Notice that $-9-18=-27,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $8\\,|\\,(21-45).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(21-45)÷8=-24÷8=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf0a248-db5e-5429-b19f-8baec1eccfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409389} {:answer/id #uuid "a563bf47-26ea-5012-bc40-ac94dd282a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409392} {:answer/id #uuid "ab09181d-d458-54be-b834-2e63ecb92ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409395} {:answer/id #uuid "ac52b52e-9b59-55a6-aee5-bdcca2c19458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409397} {:answer/id #uuid "ecaef5e2-552f-53c9-8afa-1e06d0245904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab09181d-d458-54be-b834-2e63ecb92ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409395} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c0b011b-f8f7-5378-93a8-0f3125f9e17e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b17c24c5-e750-5126-8785-6318018ca783" :question/math-academy-id "q-76959" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $-7≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $6≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $3∤(-5-11).$ Notice that $-5-11=-16,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $7\\,|\\,(-7-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-0)÷7=-7÷7=-7\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $9∤(6-25).$ Notice that $6-25=-19,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26eed0b8-bca8-534f-9777-f36e6dec7b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409450} {:answer/id #uuid "3d952b6c-e25b-5f69-9d2a-ff0c63a4d7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409456} {:answer/id #uuid "56cc2837-afdc-5789-99d5-e022a2cce2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409457} {:answer/id #uuid "b2848211-e932-5634-bc25-53a4c7b4a5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409464} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cbf23b-c664-5d3c-87b3-cd51f24d52ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d952b6c-e25b-5f69-9d2a-ff0c63a4d7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409456} :answer-field/id #uuid "79213bec-cbc2-5e7b-a011-da32a4e25d22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3936dcf8-3c18-5d25-b1ef-fee251049b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98480" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17$\n- $\\text{gcd}(765,221)=1$\n- $765$ and $221$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}765 & = & 221\\cdot 3 & + & 102 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 221 & = & 102\\cdot 2 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 102 & = & 17\\cdot 6 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $17.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $765$ and $221$ is $17:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(765,221)=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{gcd}(765,221)>1,$ the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22a76842-1f68-5a89-9f31-4d47c4cc64a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409449} {:answer/id #uuid "7670efc5-9bf9-5b1e-bd6d-fa7dd6519896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409460} {:answer/id #uuid "be5dedfb-bad9-5066-aef5-f2053df360b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409465} {:answer/id #uuid "c6752fc0-b3b1-57e7-8e8f-82c9abd8bb42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409467} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a86ba-3ae6-5111-b287-2f20bc390233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea5a86ba-3ae6-5111-b287-2f20bc390233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409472} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab5c137-e686-5330-940a-11463985a910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e2b9768-6303-5aa7-8a02-db48065ae94a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98476" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1$\n- $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=3$\n- $5719$ and $2669$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}5719 & = & 2669\\cdot 2 & + & 381 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2669 & = & 381\\cdot 7 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 381 & = & 2\\cdot 190 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 2 & = & 1\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is $1.$ So, the greatest common divisor of $5719$ and $2669$ is $1:$\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{gcd}(5719,2669)=1,$ the numbers are coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4257b911-5988-5866-b9ff-6170b641f678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409543} {:answer/id #uuid "673d57e2-c509-53cd-86d7-a568bf1e97f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409546} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6aa6de-7d86-5660-b9a6-8496a3ca7253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409548} {:answer/id #uuid "c44c870f-1d5b-5d9f-93d5-da63006f4e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409550} {:answer/id #uuid "d4667459-8749-5c76-8a2a-d3b084c75e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4667459-8749-5c76-8a2a-d3b084c75e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409551} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c2354d-85e6-577f-a722-6c718f129147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-132726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3},1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}_{y}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is also true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{1})=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}]}
